        </param>
        <param>
          <type>drogon::orm::Result::SizeType</type>
          <declname>row</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Converts one row into a JSON object using name- and value-based type inference. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Maps one result row to a JSON object keyed by column name.</para>
<para>Each row gets its own object. Parse failures are swallowed and the raw text is kept, so this function never throws for a malformed integer or JSON value. Field values are read through their C-string text form.</para>
<para>Conversion order: SQL NULL to JSON null; a column named <computeroutput>active</computeroutput> to a boolean (<computeroutput>t</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>true</computeroutput> or <computeroutput>1</computeroutput> mean true); <computeroutput>id</computeroutput>, names ending in <computeroutput>_id</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>appointment_duration</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>appointment_payment_amount</computeroutput> to an integer when <computeroutput>std::stoi</computeroutput> succeeds; a non-empty value starting with <computeroutput>{</computeroutput> or <computeroutput>[</computeroutput> to parsed JSON; otherwise a string. Both integer and JSON parsing failures fall back to the raw string, so a malformed value cannot abort mapping. A fresh object is built for each call, so no field can leak between rows. <parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">result</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Result set returned by the query. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">row</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Zero-based row index into <computeroutput>result</computeroutput>. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>JSON object with one entry per column, in column order. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="pre"><para><computeroutput>row</computeroutput> is a valid index into <computeroutput>result</computeroutput>. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
